DP  - 2005 Aug 19
TI  - Genome streamlining in a cosmopolitan oceanic bacterium.
PG  - 1242-5
AB  - The SAR11 clade consists of very small, heterotrophic marine alpha-proteobacteria 
      that are found throughout the oceans, where they account for about 25% of all 
      microbial cells. Pelagibacter ubique, the first cultured member of this clade, 
      has the smallest genome and encodes the smallest number of predicted open reading 
      frames known for a free-living microorganism. In contrast to parasitic bacteria 
      and archaea with small genomes, P. ubique has complete biosynthetic pathways for 
      all 20 amino acids and all but a few cofactors. P. ubique has no pseudogenes, 
      introns, transposons, extrachromosomal elements, or inteins; few paralogs; and 
      the shortest intergenic spacers yet observed for any cell.
