TI  - Bacterial taxa that limit sulfur flux from the ocean.
PG  - 649-52
AB  - Flux of dimethylsulfide (DMS) from ocean surface waters is the predominant 
      natural source of sulfur to the atmosphere and influences climate by aerosol 
      formation. Marine bacterioplankton regulate sulfur flux by converting the 
      precursor dimethylsulfoniopropionate (DMSP) either to DMS or to sulfur compounds 
      that are not climatically active. Through the discovery of a glycine cleavage 
      T-family protein with DMSP methyltransferase activity, marine bacterioplankton in 
      the Roseobacter and SAR11 taxa were identified as primary mediators of DMSP 
      demethylation to methylmercaptopropionate. One-third of surface ocean bacteria 
      harbor a DMSP demethylase homolog and thereby route a substantial fraction of 
      global marine primary production away from DMS formation and into the marine 
      microbial food web.
