DP  - 2015 Nov
TI  - The ocean as a global reservoir of antibiotic resistance genes.
PG  - 7593-9
LID - 10.1128/AEM.00736-15 [doi]
AB  - Recent studies of natural environments have revealed vast genetic reservoirs of 
      antibiotic resistance (AR) genes. Soil bacteria and human pathogens share AR 
      genes, and AR genes have been discovered in a variety of habitats. However, there 
      is little knowledge about the presence and diversity of AR genes in marine 
      environments and which organisms host AR genes. To address this, we identified 
      the diversity of genes conferring resistance to ampicillin, tetracycline, 
      nitrofurantoin, and sulfadimethoxine in diverse marine environments using 
      functional metagenomics (the cloning and screening of random DNA fragments). 
      Marine environments were host to a diversity of AR-conferring genes. 
      Antibiotic-resistant clones were found at all sites, with 28% of the genes 
      identified as known AR genes (encoding beta-lactamases, bicyclomycin resistance 
      pumps, etc.). However, the majority of AR genes were not previously classified as 
      such but had products similar to proteins such as transport pumps, 
      oxidoreductases, and hydrolases. Furthermore, 44% of the genes conferring 
      antibiotic resistance were found in abundant marine taxa (e.g., Pelagibacter, 
      Prochlorococcus, and Vibrio). Therefore, we uncovered a previously unknown 
      diversity of genes that conferred an AR phenotype among marine environments, 
      which makes the ocean a global reservoir of both clinically relevant and 
      potentially novel AR genes.
